  "Question": "The solution of \\(\\sqrt{5x-1}+\\sqrt{x-1}=2\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x=2,\\) \\(x=1\\)",
    "B": "\\(x=\\dfrac23\\)",
    "C": "\\(x=2\\)",
    "D": "\\(x=1\\)",
    "E": "\\(x=0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The radical \\(\\sqrt{x-1}\\) requires \\(x\\ge1\\). This also ensures that \\(5x-1\\ge4\\), so throughout the real domain,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5x-1}\\ge2,\\qquad \\sqrt{x-1}\\ge0.\n\\]\nAt \\(x=1\\), the original equation becomes\n\\[\n\\sqrt4+\\sqrt0=2,\n\\]\nso \\(1\\) is a solution. If \\(x>1\\), then \\(5x-1>4\\), making the first radical greater than \\(2\\); adding the nonnegative second radical cannot give \\(2\\).\n\nTherefore the unique solution is \\(\\boxed{x=1}\\), choice D.",
